<section class="chapter" id="purpose">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">1. Purpose &amp; Outcomes</h2>

      <p class="muted">
        Most task trackers fail at the moment of execution: the day starts, meetings happen, energy shifts —
        and the list becomes noise. MyPlanner exists to convert a tracker into a <strong>decision-ready morning plan</strong>:
        what matters today, when it happens, and what to say when you need to move work forward.
      </p>

      <div class="miniGrid" aria-label="Outcome pillars">
        <div class="mini">
          <b>Less drift</b>
          <span>A tight “Today’s Focus” prevents reactive work from eating the day.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="mini">
          <b>Fewer open loops</b>
          <span>Draft messages + targets reduce the friction of follow-ups and handoffs.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="mini">
          <b>Execution-ready</b>
          <span>Time blocks with buffers assume real life (20% overrun) so plans survive contact.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Generic by design:</strong> swap “application tracker” for any <em>task tracker</em> sheet.
        MyPlanner detects common columns (status, priority, next step, last touch, notes, links) and works with what it finds.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="breaks">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">2. What Breaks Without This</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Task lists grow, but priorities stay implicit — everything feels “urgent.”</li>
        <li>Follow-ups happen late because the “next step” isn’t surfaced at the start of the day.</li>
        <li>Calendar reality and task reality are disconnected (meetings steal the execution window).</li>
        <li>You spend peak-energy time deciding what to do instead of doing it.</li>
        <li>Plans don’t compound because the system never captures “Where we left off.”</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="does">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">3. What It Does</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Reads</strong> a tracker sheet (first tab) and keeps active/recent items (e.g., active statuses or recent touches).</li>
        <li><strong>Reads</strong> today’s calendar and turns it into a realistic block schedule.</li>
        <li><strong>Generates</strong> a structured plan with consistent headers (Focus → Top 5 → Schedule → Draft messages → Targets).</li>
        <li><strong>Writes</strong> the day’s plan into a running Google Doc (upsert by date so re-runs replace today cleanly).</li>
        <li><strong>Normalizes</strong> formatting (H2/H3 + base font) so the doc stays readable over time.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="small">
        If an OpenAI key isn’t present, MyPlanner still produces a deterministic fallback plan — so the workflow never blocks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="how">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">4. How It Works</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Google Sheet (task tracker), Google Calendar (today), Script Properties (optional <code>OPENAI_KEY</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>Planner engine:</strong> prompt builder + robust section parser (so headers don’t drift).</li>
        <li><strong>Output:</strong> Google Doc “My Planner — Control Panel” with one H2 section per day.</li>
        <li><strong>Controls:</strong> custom Sheets menu (“Run Daily Plan now”, “Reset doc &amp; write today”).</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Privacy-first default:</strong> the tool runs in your Google environment.
        If you enable the LLM call, only the summarized tracker bullets + today’s calendar bullets are sent to the model.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="data">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">5. Data Model (Tracker)</h2>

      <p class="muted">
        The tracker is intentionally lightweight. MyPlanner looks for common fields and gracefully degrades when some columns
        don’t exist. The goal is: <strong>enough structure to plan</strong>, not a rigid schema that breaks adoption.
      </p>

      <h3>5.1 What the planner needs (minimum viable)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Task / Item</strong> (a short title)</li>
        <li><strong>Status</strong> (to determine active vs parked vs done)</li>
        <li><strong>Priority</strong> (or equivalent signal: “P0/P1”, “High/Med/Low”, etc.)</li>
        <li><strong>Next step</strong> (so execution can start immediately)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>5.2 Helpful (improves plan quality)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Last touch</strong> (date) to surface stale follow-ups</li>
        <li><strong>Owner / stakeholder</strong> (who needs a nudge)</li>
        <li><strong>Link</strong> (doc, ticket, email thread)</li>
        <li><strong>Notes</strong> (context crumbs)</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Selection logic (default):</strong> keep active items, plus recently touched items, plus anything marked urgent/priority —
        then cap output so the plan stays executable.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="plan">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">6. Plan Format (Doc Output)</h2>

      <p class="muted">
        Consistency is the feature. MyPlanner writes the same structure every day so your brain doesn’t re-learn the doc.
        The plan is written into a single Google Doc as an H2 section per date (upsert by date).
      </p>

      <h3>6.1 Standard sections</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Section</th>
              <th>Purpose</th>
              <th>Typical Contents</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Today’s Focus</strong></td>
              <td>One sentence that prevents drift</td>
              <td>Theme / outcome for the day</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Top 5</strong></td>
              <td>The executable list</td>
              <td>5 tasks with next steps</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Schedule</strong></td>
              <td>Reality-based blocks</td>
              <td>Calendar + work blocks + buffers</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Draft Messages</strong></td>
              <td>Close open loops fast</td>
              <td>Follow-ups, handoffs, asks</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Targets</strong></td>
              <td>Measurable completion</td>
              <td>“Ship X”, “Send Y”, “Close Z”</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Header guardrail:</strong> MyPlanner parses by known headers and rewrites in a controlled format to prevent “section drift”
        over time (especially when an LLM is enabled).
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="guardrails">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">7. Guardrails &amp; Safe Defaults</h2>

      <h3>7.1 Planning guardrails</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cap the plan</strong> (avoid 20-item “plans” that aren’t plans).</li>
        <li><strong>Assume overrun</strong> (buffer time so the plan survives meetings and context switches).</li>
        <li><strong>Prefer next steps</strong> over vague goals (every item should have an action verb).</li>
        <li><strong>Surface stale items</strong> (last-touch logic so follow-ups don’t decay invisibly).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>7.2 Runtime guardrails</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Deterministic fallback</strong> when no LLM key exists (never block the workflow).</li>
        <li><strong>Upsert by date</strong> (re-runs replace today cleanly; no duplicate sections).</li>
        <li><strong>Safe parsing</strong> (ignore unknown sections; keep output stable).</li>
        <li><strong>Read-only posture</strong> to your systems: it reads Sheets/Calendar and writes only to the designated Doc.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Principle:</strong> the plan should reduce decisions, not add a new tool to manage.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="privacy">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">8. Privacy &amp; Data Minimization</h2>

      <p class="muted">
        The default mode keeps everything inside Google (Sheets + Calendar + Docs).
        If you enable the LLM call, MyPlanner minimizes what it sends: summarized bullets, not raw sheets or full event bodies.
      </p>

      <h3>8.1 Recommended defaults</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Send only: task titles + next steps + priority + last touch (as short bullets)</li>
        <li>Send only: calendar blocks (start/end + short label)</li>
        <li>Exclude: personal notes, sensitive content, or long free-text fields unless explicitly enabled</li>
        <li>Keep an audit line in the output noting whether LLM mode was enabled</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Reminder:</strong> if your tracker contains sensitive information, keep LLM mode off — the deterministic planner still works.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="ops">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">9. Operating Cadence</h2>

      <h3>9.1 Daily</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Morning run:</strong> generate plan before the first meeting block.</li>
        <li><strong>Midday re-run (optional):</strong> if meetings changed, re-run to re-time-block.</li>
        <li><strong>End-of-day:</strong> mark outcomes and capture “where we left off” in notes (feeds tomorrow).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>9.2 Weekly</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Review stale items and close/park anything that’s not real.</li>
        <li>Adjust status vocabulary so “active” means active (not “maybe someday”).</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Day drift rule:</strong> if the plan is not re-runnable, it will break. Re-runs are a feature, not a failure.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapter" id="fit">
  <div class="panel panel--cool">
    <div class="chapter__inner">
      <h2 class="section-title">10. Where It Fits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Downstream of work signals:</strong> tasks, priorities, last-touch dates, and calendar commitments.</li>
        <li><strong>Upstream of execution:</strong> outreach, writing, building, follow-ups, and closing loops.</li>
        <li><strong>Feeds other agents:</strong> planning output can become inputs for routing, audits, or portfolio ops.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

